"""Self-contained group-theoretic extensions to the proof manuscript."""

def append_extensions(p, eq, h, sub, page):
    page('10. Finite presentation over a finitely presented algebra')
    p('<b>Theorem 10.1.</b> <i>If R is a finitely presented unital associative F<sub>p</sub>-algebra and n ≥ 5, then St<sub>n</sub>(R) is finitely presented as an abstract group.</i>')
    p('Write <i>T</i> = F<sub>p</sub>⟨Σ⟩ and <i>R</i> = <i>T</i>/(<i>f</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>f</i><sub>s</sub>), with finite Σ and finitely many polynomial relators. Include the empty word ε among the monomials. Start with generators <i>g</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>) for <i>i</i> ≠ <i>j</i> and |<i>w</i>| ≤ 2. Impose the following finite families:')
    eq('<i>g</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>)<sup>p</sup> = 1;')
    eq('[<i>g</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>u</i>), <i>g</i><sub>kl</sub>(<i>v</i>)] = 1<br/>if |<i>u</i>|, |<i>v</i>| ≤ 2, &nbsp; <i>j</i> ≠ <i>k</i>, &nbsp; <i>i</i> ≠ <i>l</i>;')
    eq('[<i>g</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>u</i>), <i>g</i><sub>jk</sub>(<i>v</i>)] = <i>g</i><sub>ik</sub>(<i>uv</i>)<br/>if |<i>u</i>| + |<i>v</i>| ≤ 2, &nbsp; <i>i, j, k</i> distinct.')
    p('Define a group word ξ<sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>) recursively. At length at most two it is the displayed generator. Otherwise write <i>w</i> = <i>av</i> with first letter <i>a</i>, choose the least index <i>h</i> outside {<i>i,j</i>}, and set ξ<sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>) = [ξ<sub>ih</sub>(<i>a</i>), ξ<sub>hj</sub>(<i>v</i>)]. For <i>f</i><sub>t</sub> = ∑<sub>w</sub> <i>c</i><sub>tw</sub><i>w</i>, add')
    eq('∏<sub>w</sub> ξ<sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>)<sup>c<sub>tw</sub></sup> = 1 &nbsp; (1 ≤ <i>t</i> ≤ <i>s</i>, &nbsp; <i>i</i> ≠ <i>j</i>).')
    p('The coefficients are represented by integers from 0 to <i>p</i> − 1, and the finite product is initially taken in any fixed monomial order. We prove that this presentation gives St<sub>n</sub>(<i>R</i>).')
    sub('A commutator identity used in the induction')
    p('Let <i>A</i> = [<i>X,Y</i>] and <i>B</i> = [<i>Y,Z</i>]. Suppose <i>A</i> commutes with <i>X,Y,B</i>, <i>B</i> commutes with <i>Y,Z</i>, and <i>X</i> commutes with <i>Z</i>. Then [<i>A,Z</i>] = [<i>X,B</i>], and this common element centralizes <i>A,Z,X,B</i>. Indeed, ⟨<i>A,Z</i>⟩ and ⟨<i>X,B</i>⟩ centralize one another. Conjugating [<i>X,Z</i>] = 1 by <i>Y</i> yields [<i>A</i><sup>−1</sup>,<i>Z</i>][<i>X,B</i>] = 1. The two factors lie in the intersection of these commuting subgroups and are central there. Thus [<i>A</i><sup>−1</sup>,<i>Z</i>] = [<i>A,Z</i>]<sup>−1</sup>, proving the assertion.')

    page('10. Finite presentation (continued)')
    sub('Induction on monomial length')
    p('Temporarily omit the polynomial relators and call the presented group <i>P</i>. At stage <i>N</i> construct roots <i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>) for |<i>w</i>| ≤ <i>N</i>, proving simultaneously: independence of all positive splits and intermediate indices; order dividing <i>p</i>; all commuting-position relations between these roots; and every consecutive-root relation of total length at most <i>N</i>, including empty-word factors. The initial stage <i>N</i> = 2 is exactly the defining relations.')
    p('At a new length <i>N</i> ≥ 3 consider [<i>y</i><sub>ih</sub>(<i>u</i>), <i>y</i><sub>hj</sub>(<i>v</i>)] for every positive split <i>w</i> = <i>uv</i>. For three nonempty pieces <i>u,v,z</i> and four distinct indices <i>i,a,b,j</i>, the preceding identity gives')
    eq('[<i>y</i><sub>ib</sub>(<i>uv</i>), <i>y</i><sub>bj</sub>(<i>z</i>)]<br/>= [<i>y</i><sub>ia</sub>(<i>u</i>), <i>y</i><sub>aj</sub>(<i>vz</i>)].')
    p('Every hypothesis of that identity concerns roots of smaller individual length, in commuting positions, and is already proved. Regard a candidate as a pair (cut position, intermediate index). Candidates with different cuts and different indices are equal by this formula. The resulting graph is connected: there are at least two cuts and at least three available intermediate indices. For equal cuts, pass through a different cut and a third index; for equal indices, use two other indices and alternate the two cuts. Hence all candidates agree and define <i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>).')
    p('Next prove all commuting-position relations involving length <i>N</i>, by increasing total length from <i>N</i> to 2<i>N</i>. For [<i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>u</i>), <i>y</i><sub>kl</sub>(<i>v</i>)] with |<i>u</i>| = <i>N</i>, choose <i>h</i> outside {<i>i,j,k,l</i>} and split <i>u</i> = <i>u</i><sub>1</sub><i>u</i><sub>2</sub> positively. Both factors of [<i>y</i><sub>ih</sub>(<i>u</i><sub>1</sub>), <i>y</i><sub>hj</sub>(<i>u</i><sub>2</sub>)] commute with the other root by a smaller total length. Their commutator does also. This establishes the whole commuting family before any further new-length argument.')
    p('If the new root is <i>C</i> = [<i>U,V</i>], these commutations say that <i>C</i> centralizes <i>U,V</i>. Since <i>U</i><sup>p</sup> = 1 at a smaller length, the identity [<i>U</i><sup>p</sup>,<i>V</i>] = <i>C</i><sup>p</sup> gives <i>C</i><sup>p</sup> = 1. This argument works for every prime, with no involution assumption.')
    p('For an empty factor on the right, write <i>w</i> = <i>uv</i> positively and choose <i>h</i> outside {<i>i,j,k</i>}. Apply the commutator identity to <i>X</i> = <i>y</i><sub>ih</sub>(<i>u</i>), <i>Y</i> = <i>y</i><sub>hj</sub>(<i>v</i>), <i>Z</i> = <i>y</i><sub>jk</sub>(ε). The smaller-length relation for [<i>Y,Z</i>] and positive-split independence give [<i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>), <i>y</i><sub>jk</sub>(ε)] = <i>y</i><sub>ik</sub>(<i>w</i>). For an empty factor on the left use <i>X</i> = <i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(ε), <i>Y</i> = <i>y</i><sub>jh</sub>(<i>u</i>), <i>Z</i> = <i>y</i><sub>hk</sub>(<i>v</i>). This closes the induction.')

    page('10. Finite presentation (completion)')
    p('For a polynomial <i>f</i> = ∑ <i>c</i><sub>w</sub><i>w</i>, define <i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>f</i>) = ∏ <i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>w</i>)<sup>c<sub>w</sub></sup>. Same-position roots commute and have exponent dividing <i>p</i>, so this is additive in <i>f</i> over F<sub>p</sub>. The other commuting relations extend factor by factor. For consecutive positions, every resulting monomial commutator commutes with both input products, so expansion gives')
    eq('[<i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>f</i>), <i>y</i><sub>jk</sub>(<i>g</i>)] = <i>y</i><sub>ik</sub>(<i>fg</i>).')
    p('Thus these elements define St<sub>n</sub>(<i>T</i>) → <i>P</i>. The reverse map sends each original generator to its monomial root. Recursion and additivity show that the two maps fix all generators and are inverse.')
    p('Imposing the polynomial relators kills the roots of every element of their two-sided ideal. Indeed, if the roots of <i>f</i><sub>t</sub> are killed in every position, consecutive commutators first kill roots of <i>af</i><sub>t</sub> and then of <i>af</i><sub>t</sub><i>b</i>. Additivity kills their finite sums. Consequently root parameters depend only on their class in <i>R</i>, and the quotient presentation is exactly St<sub>n</sub>(<i>R</i>). This proves Theorem 10.1. □')
    p('Theorem 18.1 supplies the finite algebra presentation needed here. Finite presentation is not inferred from grading or matrix nilpotence alone.')

    page('11. Local rings, strips, and centrality')
    p('In Sections 11-13 assume <i>B</i> = F<sub>p</sub> ⊕ <i>K</i>, where <i>K</i> is an ideal and every finite matrix over <i>K</i> is nilpotent. A matrix is invertible precisely when its reduction over F<sub>p</sub> is invertible: lift the inverse of the reduction and invert the remaining matrix 1 + <i>A</i> by its finite geometric series. In particular, the nonunits of <i>B</i> are exactly <i>K</i>. Independent reduced columns extend to an invertible matrix by lifting a complementary basis.')
    p('Put <i>H</i><sub>n</sub> = GL<sub>n</sub>(<i>B</i>) and <i>J</i><sub>n</sub>(<i>B</i>) = ker π<sub>n</sub>. Row and column elimination using an invertible pivot shows that <i>H</i><sub>n</sub> is generated by elementary matrices and matrices <i>d</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>u</i>) with a unit in one diagonal position. A literal row interchange is a product of elementary matrices and a diagonal sign. This observation keeps the argument valid when <i>p</i> is odd.')
    sub('A presentation supported on at most five coordinates')
    p('<b>Lemma 11.1.</b> <i>For n ≥ 5, H<sub>n</sub> is presented by these generators and all true matrix relations supported on at most five coordinates.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> The complex of columns with independent reductions is simply connected for <i>n</i> ≥ 3. To verify this, first take a finite labeled spanning collection over the field. Begin with a basis simplex and add labels. The link of an added vector is the independence complex of the preceding vectors projected modulo that vector, with zero projections discarded. Induction on rank gives vanishing reduced homology through rank minus two, using the relative chains of the attached cone. In rank at least three the links are connected. Attaching their cones preserves simple connectivity by replacing paths through a cone with paths in its link. Any loop in the full column complex lies in such a finite collection after standard basis columns have been added.')
    p('Let <i>H</i>′<sub>n</sub> be the group in the claimed presentation. Induct on <i>n</i>, starting with <i>n</i> = 5, where every matrix relation is included. The stabilizer <i>P</i> of the first standard column is a row strip semidirect GL<sub>n−1</sub>(<i>B</i>). By induction its complementary block lifts to <i>H</i>′<sub>n</sub>; the strip and its conjugation laws involve at most three coordinates and also lift. Hence <i>P</i> has an injective section <i>P</i>′. Let <i>s</i> be the literal swap of the first two columns, expressed using the allowed elementary and diagonal generators. Then <i>P</i>′ and <i>s</i> generate <i>H</i>′<sub>n</sub>.')
    p('Transport a coset <i>hP</i>′ along an edge from its column to a neighboring column by <i>hP</i>′ ↦ <i>h</i>p̃<i>sP</i>′, where <i>p</i> ∈ <i>P</i> sends the second standard column to the prescribed neighbor in <i>h</i>-coordinates. The subgroup fixing the first two columns is generated by the remaining complementary block and the two row strips. The swap normalizes its lift by relations on at most three coordinates. Therefore transport is independent of the chosen <i>p</i> and of the coset representative.')
    p('Backtracking is trivial since <i>s</i><sup>2</sup> = 1. A triangle can be carried to the first three standard columns. Writing <i>t</i> for their last-two swap, its successive representatives are <i>s</i>, <i>sts</i>, <i>ststs</i> = <i>t</i> ∈ <i>P</i>′; this is a three-coordinate matrix relation. Simple connectivity makes transport depend only on the endpoint. Every coset is reached because <i>P</i>′ and <i>s</i> generate. Thus the cosets correspond bijectively to columns. An element in the kernel of <i>H</i>′<sub>n</sub> → <i>H</i><sub>n</sub> lies in <i>P</i>′, where the map is injective. □')

    page('11. Centrality and stable quotients')
    p('<b>Lemma 11.2.</b> <i>For n ≥ 6 the kernel J<sub>n</sub>(B) is central. For n ≥ 5 the quotients GL<sub>n</sub>(B)/E<sub>n</sub>(B) are unchanged by stabilization.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Disjoint sets of coordinates <i>S,T</i> determine an additive abelian strip generated by x<sub>st</sub>(<i>a</i>), <i>s</i> ∈ <i>S</i>, <i>t</i> ∈ <i>T</i>. Its matrix map is injective, since products within the strip have zero cross terms. Conjugation by roots supported on <i>S</i> or <i>T</i> acts by the exact corresponding row or inverse column operation. This follows from consecutive-root relations; the reversed consecutive commutator carries a minus sign.')
    p('Assign to an elementary matrix conjugation by its root, and to a diagonal matrix the automorphism x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>a</i>) ↦ x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>u</i><sub>i</sub><i>a</i><i>u</i><sub>j</sub><sup>−1</sup>). These preserve root relations. Consider a true matrix relation supported on at most five coordinates, and choose one coordinate <i>t</i> outside them. Its action on the incoming and outgoing strips is respectively the action of its matrix and its inverse, so it fixes both strips. It fixes roots outside the support and, by x<sub>ij</sub>(<i>a</i>) = [x<sub>it</sub>(<i>a</i>),x<sub>tj</sub>(1)], also the roots inside. Lemma 11.1 therefore makes this an action of GL<sub>n</sub>(<i>B</i>) on St<sub>n</sub>(<i>B</i>). The action of π<sub>n</sub>(<i>z</i>) is conjugation by <i>z</i>, so an identity matrix image implies centrality.')
    p('Elementary and diagonal generators normalize E<sub>n</sub>(<i>B</i>), proving normality. To compute the quotient, kill the elementary generators in Lemma 11.1. For arbitrary characteristic the elementary signed interchange')
    eq('e<sub>ij</sub>(1)e<sub>ji</sub>(−1)e<sub>ij</sub>(1)<br/>has block &nbsp; ((0, 1), (−1, 0)).')
    p('conjugates <i>d</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>u</i>) to <i>d</i><sub>j</sub>(<i>u</i>); the signs are central scalars. Thus all diagonal positions have the same class <i>d</i>(<i>u</i>). Diagonals in distinct positions commute. The remaining relations are exactly the true relations supported on at most five coordinates with elementary factors deleted and diagonal positions identified. This list is independent of <i>n</i> ≥ 5, proving stabilization of the quotient. □')
    p('<b>Lemma 11.3.</b> <i>For every unital ring B, the stable matrix kernel J(B) is central. Coordinate re-embeddings fix its elements.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> If a root word <i>z</i> has identity matrix image, take a spare coordinate outside its support. The faithful incoming and outgoing strips show that <i>z</i> commutes with all roots involving that coordinate. Express any internal root as a consecutive commutator through the spare coordinate. Repeat for any other external coordinate; disjoint roots already commute. Thus <i>z</i> is central. To re-embed its occupied coordinates, first transfer them one by one to a disjoint temporary set and then to the desired set. Each transfer uses an elementary signed interchange sending the occupied basis vector to an unused basis vector with coefficient +1. No root in the current word uses both interchanged coordinates, so the exact strip action proves the required conjugation on every root; the minus sign affects only the previously unused coordinate. Centrality fixes <i>z</i> throughout. □')

    page('12. Injectivity at a fixed rank')
    p('<b>Theorem 12.1.</b> <i>For B as in Section 11 and n ≥ 12, the homomorphism St<sub>n</sub>(B) → St(B) is injective.</i>')
    sub('Central extensions of frame stabilizers')
    p('For <i>r</i> ≥ 6 and <i>l</i> ≥ 0 let <i>S</i><sub>r,l</sub> be the matrices ((<i>A</i>,0),(<i>C</i>,1<sub>l</sub>)), with <i>A</i> ∈ E<sub>r</sub>(<i>B</i>) and arbitrary <i>l</i> by <i>r</i> strip <i>C</i>. Replace the elementary block by St<sub>r</sub>(<i>B</i>), acting through its matrix image, to obtain S̃<sub>r,l</sub>. The map S̃<sub>r,l</sub> → <i>S</i><sub>r,l</sub> is a central extension with kernel <i>J</i><sub>r</sub>(<i>B</i>) by Lemma 11.2. Both groups are perfect: every internal or strip root is a consecutive commutator through an internal coordinate.')
    p('This is the universal central extension. Here is the root-lifting verification. In any central extension <i>V</i> → <i>S</i><sub>r,l</sub>, choose lifts <i>y</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>a</i>) of all available roots, whose column index is internal. Lifts in commuting root positions commute exactly. Indeed, express one of their matrix images as a consecutive commutator through a spare internal coordinate. Each factor commutes with the other matrix root, so its commutator with a lift is central. Conjugation by the other lift fixes the commutator of those factors. That commutator differs from the chosen lift only by a central element.')
    p('Correct the lifts by setting ŷ<sub>ij</sub>(<i>a</i>) = [<i>y</i><sub>ih</sub>(<i>a</i>),<i>y</i><sub>hj</sub>(1)]. The identity in Section 10, with an additional internal coordinate, gives')
    eq('[<i>y</i><sub>ih</sub>(<i>a</i>), <i>y</i><sub>hj</sub>(<i>b</i>)] = ŷ<sub>ij</sub>(<i>ab</i>).')
    p('To check this explicitly use <i>X</i> = <i>y</i><sub>ih</sub>(<i>a</i>), <i>Y</i> = <i>y</i><sub>hf</sub>(<i>b</i>), <i>Z</i> = <i>y</i><sub>fj</sub>(1), replacing resulting root lifts by their central multiples. Taking <i>b</i> = 1 first proves independence of <i>h</i>. Expanding the corrected lift of <i>a</i> + <i>a</i>′ proves additivity, since the resulting commutators centralize the input factors. Thus the corrected roots satisfy all Steinberg relations and the exact action on the strip. They give a homomorphism S̃<sub>r,l</sub> → <i>V</i>. It is unique because its source is perfect and the difference between two lifts is a homomorphism to an abelian central kernel.')
    p('We use integral group homology, computed by the bar resolution. For a perfect group the kernel of its universal central extension identifies naturally with H<sub>2</sub>. One algebraic description is this: if <i>F</i>/<i>N</i> is a free presentation, the extension [<i>F,F</i>]/[<i>F,N</i>] → <i>F</i>/<i>N</i> is universal and its kernel is (<i>N</i> ∩ [<i>F,F</i>])/[<i>F,N</i>], the degree-two homology group. The universal property follows by lifting free generators and observing that changes of lifts are central; the same kernel formula is obtained by resolving the free presentation in degrees one and two. In particular the block inclusion induces natural isomorphisms')
    eq('H<sub>2</sub>(E<sub>r</sub>(<i>B</i>); ℤ) ≅ <i>J</i><sub>r</sub>(<i>B</i>) ≅ H<sub>2</sub>(<i>S</i><sub>r,l</sub>; ℤ).')

    page('12. Ordered frames and their homology')
    p('Let <i>C</i><sub>a</sub>(<i>m</i>) be the free abelian group on ordered independent (<i>a</i> + 1)-tuples of reduced columns of <i>B</i><sup>m</sup>, retaining the original columns as labels. Its differential deletes entries with alternating signs. Every ordering is a separate generator; no orientation relation identifies two orderings. Augment by <i>C</i><sub>−1</sub> = ℤ.')
    p('<b>Lemma 12.2.</b> <i>This augmented complex is exact through degree m − 2.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> For a finite labeled spanning collection <i>V</i> over the field, induct on its number of labels, allowing rank zero. Delete a label <i>v</i>, obtaining <i>V</i>′. In the relative complex the tuples contain <i>v</i>. Filter by the number <i>a</i> of entries preceding <i>v</i>. For a fixed ordered prefix of length <i>a</i> + 1, the associated graded summand is the augmented ordered-frame complex of the remaining vectors projected modulo that prefix, shifted by <i>a</i> + 1. Their span has dimension <i>m</i> − <i>a</i> − 1. Induction gives vanishing through degree (<i>m</i> − <i>a</i> − 3) + (<i>a</i> + 1) = <i>m</i> − 2. If this quotient has dimension zero, its sole homology shifts to degree <i>m</i> − 1, outside that range. A finite filtration gives the same relative vanishing.')
    p('If deletion preserves rank, the long exact sequence and induction finish the proof. Otherwise <i>v</i> is independent of the span of <i>V</i>′. Prepending <i>v</i> is a chain homotopy killing the inclusion of its augmented complex. That inclusion is surjective on homology in the stated range by relative vanishing, hence the target homology is zero. Finally every cycle of the full column complex lies in a finite collection; adjoining the standard basis columns makes it spanning. □')
    sub('The comparison of successive ranks')
    p('Fix <i>m</i> ≥ 13. For frames of length <i>l</i> ≤ 5, E<sub>m</sub>(<i>B</i>) acts transitively. Complete the frame in the last <i>l</i> columns to an invertible matrix. Lemma 11.2 lets a complementary GL<sub>m−l</sub> block cancel its class modulo E<sub>m</sub>, while leaving the frame unchanged. Its stabilizer is <i>S</i><sub>m−l,l</sub>: the same quotient injectivity forces its complementary block to lie in E<sub>m−l</sub>.')
    p('Take a free right ℤ[E<sub>m</sub>]-resolution <i>P</i><sub>•</sub> → ℤ and the double complex <i>P</i><sub>b</sub> ⊗ <i>C</i><sub>a</sub>(<i>m</i>), with <i>a,b</i> ≥ 0. Adding the augmentation column and using Lemma 12.2 shows that augmentation identifies total homology in degree two with H<sub>2</sub>(E<sub>m</sub>;ℤ). Taking vertical homology first gives, for 0 ≤ <i>a</i> ≤ 4,')
    eq('<i>E</i><sup>1</sup><sub>a,b</sub> = H<sub>b</sub>(<i>S</i><sub>m−a−1,a+1</sub>; ℤ).')
    p('This identification follows directly because the permutation module of a transitive frame set is the module induced from its stabilizer; restricting the free resolution computes that stabilizer’s homology. The first differential is the alternating sum of the face inclusions, after returning each face to the standard frame.')

    page('12. Completion of the stability argument')
    p('The row <i>b</i> = 1 vanishes in these columns, since the stabilizers are perfect. The row <i>b</i> = 0 consists of ℤ with every face map the identity, so its differential is the identity for positive even <i>a</i> and zero for odd <i>a</i>. Consequently')
    eq('<i>E</i><sup>2</sup><sub>1,1</sub> = <i>E</i><sup>2</sup><sub>2,1</sub> = <i>E</i><sup>2</sup><sub>2,0</sub> = <i>E</i><sup>2</sup><sub>3,0</sub> = 0.')
    p('In row <i>b</i> = 2, the two faces from column one to column zero induce the same map. By the central-extension computation it suffices to compare them on the first <i>m</i> − 2 coordinates. One face already gives the last standard column. To return the other to it, use the elementary determinant-one matrix on the last two coordinates sending e<sub>m−1</sub> to e<sub>m</sub> and e<sub>m</sub> to −e<sub>m−1</sub>. This fixes the entire complementary block. Thus the two face maps coincide in every characteristic, and their alternating sum is zero.')
    eq('<i>E</i><sup>2</sup><sub>0,2</sub> = H<sub>2</sub>(<i>S</i><sub>m−1,1</sub>; ℤ).')
    p('No higher differential leaves (0,2). The only possible incoming sources are (2,1) on page two and (3,0) on page three, both zero by the displayed vanishing. All other positions of total degree two vanish as well. The filtration of total homology therefore identifies the edge map with an isomorphism')
    eq('H<sub>2</sub>(<i>S</i><sub>m−1,1</sub>; ℤ) → H<sub>2</sub>(E<sub>m</sub>(<i>B</i>); ℤ).')
    p('The edge map is induced by inclusion: its zero-th column corresponds to the last standard column, and augmentation is the stabilizer inclusion on the free resolutions. Combining with the natural central-extension identifications proves that <i>J</i><sub>m−1</sub>(<i>B</i>) → <i>J</i><sub>m</sub>(<i>B</i>) is an isomorphism. Finally, an element killed by stabilization already has identity matrix image, since adjoining identity blocks is injective on matrices. It belongs to <i>J</i><sub>n</sub> and cannot be killed at any later rank when <i>n</i> ≥ 12. The direct limit detects equality at a finite stage. This proves Theorem 12.1. □')

    page('13. Finiteness of the truncated Steinberg groups')
    p('<b>Lemma 13.1.</b> <i>A finitely generated perfect group with a central subgroup of finite index is finite.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Let <i>Z</i> be the central subgroup and let its index be <i>q</i>. A finite coset transversal rewrites generators of the ambient group into finitely many generators of <i>Z</i>, by the same telescoping argument as in Section 3; finite presentation is unnecessary here. Thus <i>Z</i> is a finitely generated abelian group.')
    p('Choose representatives <i>t</i><sub>1</sub>, …, <i>t</i><sub>q</sub> for its cosets. For an element <i>g</i>, write uniquely <i>t</i><sub>i</sub><i>g</i> = <i>z</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>g</i>)<i>t</i><sub>σg(i)</sub>, where <i>z</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>g</i>) ∈ <i>Z</i> and σ<sub>g</sub> is a permutation. Multiplication and centrality give a homomorphism')
    eq('<i>V</i>(<i>g</i>) = ∏<sub>i=1</sub><sup>q</sup> <i>z</i><sub>i</sub>(<i>g</i>) &nbsp; from the group to <i>Z</i>.')
    p('For <i>z</i> ∈ <i>Z</i> it satisfies <i>V</i>(<i>z</i>) = <i>z</i><sup>q</sup>. Perfectness makes every homomorphism to an abelian group trivial, so every element of <i>Z</i> has order dividing <i>q</i>. If <i>Z</i> has <i>r</i> generators it has at most <i>q</i><sup>r</sup> elements. Finite index now makes the original group finite. □')
    p('<b>Theorem 13.2.</b> <i>For a finite algebra B = F<sub>p</sub> ⊕ K with matrix-nil K and n ≥ 6, the group St<sub>n</sub>(B) is finite.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> There are finitely many root generators since <i>B</i> is finite. The group is perfect by Lemma 2.1, and its kernel over E<sub>n</sub>(<i>B</i>) is central by Lemma 11.2. The matrix group is finite, so this kernel has finite index. Apply Lemma 13.1. □')
    p('<b>Corollary 13.3.</b> <i>Let R = F<sub>p</sub> ⊕ I be positively graded with finite-dimensional homogeneous parts. For n ≥ 6, every St<sub>n</sub>(R<sub>d</sub>) is finite.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> Its underlying vector space is the sum of the finitely many finite-dimensional components of degrees 0 through <i>d</i> − 1. Its positive ideal has <i>d</i>th power zero. Any matrix over that ideal has <i>d</i>th power zero too, since each entry is a sum of products of <i>d</i> ideal elements. Theorem 13.2 applies, including <i>d</i> = 1. □')
    p('In particular, all truncation targets used at rank twelve are finite. The central-kernel argument is necessary in addition to finiteness of the ring.')

    page('14. Relative p-power torsion in the stable kernel')
    p('In this section <i>B</i> is any unital F<sub>p</sub>-algebra. Use outer indices <i>i,j</i> and inner indices 0 ≤ <i>s,t</i> &lt; <i>b</i>. For a matrix <i>A</i> = (<i>a</i><sub>st</sub>) define the block root')
    eq('<i>X</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>A</i>) = ∏<sub>s,t</sub> x<sub>(i,s),(j,t)</sub>(<i>a</i><sub>st</sub>) &nbsp; (<i>i</i> ≠ <i>j</i>).')
    p('Its factors commute. Consecutive block commutators multiply matrices because the only nontrivial constituent commutators have a common middle coordinate and all their output roots centralize both blocks. Together with addition and commuting positions this defines St<sub>n</sub>(M<sub>b</sub>(<i>B</i>)) → St<sub>nb</sub>(<i>B</i>).')
    p('A numerical basis change <i>P</i> ∈ GL<sub>b</sub>(F<sub>p</sub>) can be implemented simultaneously in all outer blocks in the stable group. Adjoin a spare coordinate and compensate the determinant of diag(<i>P</i>, …, <i>P</i>) there. Factor each <i>P</i> by elimination into transvections and diagonal scalings. Implement a scaling <i>u</i> with its inverse on the spare coordinate: if <i>w</i>(<i>u</i>) = e<sub>12</sub>(<i>u</i>)e<sub>21</sub>(−<i>u</i><sup>−1</sup>)e<sub>12</sub>(<i>u</i>), direct multiplication gives <i>w</i>(<i>u</i>)<i>w</i>(−1) = diag(<i>u,u</i><sup>−1</sup>). The strip actions verify at each step that the lifts conjugate block roots to <i>X</i><sub>ij</sub>(<i>P</i><sup>−1</sup><i>AP</i>). This supplies exact group conjugation in every characteristic, including determinant compensation.')
    sub('Triangular substitution')
    p('Let <i>v</i>(<i>t</i>) be a root word over <i>B</i>[<i>t</i>], where <i>t</i> is central, with identity matrix image. A numerical matrix <i>T</i> may be substituted for <i>t</i>; write <i>W</i><sub>v</sub>(<i>T</i>) for the result followed by block roots. If <i>T</i> is upper triangular with constant diagonal λ, then')
    eq('<i>W</i><sub>v</sub>(<i>T</i>) = ∏<sub>s=0</sub><sup>b−1</sup> ι<sub>s</sub>(<i>v</i>(λ)),')
    p('where ι<sub>s</sub> puts a root in the coordinates with inner index <i>s</i>. To prove this, consider the group generated by all roots of strictly positive inner gap <i>t</i> − <i>s</i>, allowing the outer indices to agree. Commutators add gaps, and opposite positions cannot occur. Collecting by increasing gap gives one coefficient at each position. If its matrix image is one, entries at the least remaining gap are exactly those coefficients, so they vanish successively. This group is faithful in matrices. Roots of gap zero normalize it, as every resulting commutator has positive gap. Split each evaluated block root into its diagonal part and positive-gap part, and collect. The remaining positive-gap factor has identity matrix image and is therefore trivial. This proves the formula.')

    page('14. Removing the wrap of a cyclic shift')
    p('Let <i>C</i><sub>b</sub> be the shift with entries 1 from row <i>s</i> to column <i>s</i> + 1 modulo <i>b</i>, and let <i>S</i><sub>b</sub> be the same shift with its final wrap entry deleted. Suppose <i>v</i>(<i>t</i>) has <i>m</i> factors and all polynomial entries have degree at most <i>d</i>. Put δ = max(1,<i>d</i>). We prove')
    eq('<i>b</i> &gt; 2(<i>m</i> + 1)δ &nbsp; ⇒ &nbsp; <i>W</i><sub>v</sub>(<i>C</i><sub>b</sub>) = <i>W</i><sub>v</sub>(<i>S</i><sub>b</sub>).')
    p('Split each cyclic block root into <i>D</i><sub>h</sub><i>F</i><sub>h</sub>, where <i>D</i><sub>h</sub> is its truncated evaluation and <i>F</i><sub>h</sub> consists of wrap entries. A degree at most <i>d</i> term wraps at most once, so every correction goes from the last δ inner coordinates to the first δ inner coordinates. These form a faithful abelian strip.')
    p('For <i>r</i> ≥ 1 let <i>V</i><sub>r</sub> be the strip from the last <i>r</i>δ inner coordinates to the first <i>r</i>δ inner coordinates, with all outer coordinates included. These sets remain disjoint up to <i>r</i> = <i>m</i> + 1. Conjugation by one <i>D</i><sub>h</sub> sends <i>V</i><sub>r</sub> into <i>V</i><sub>r+1</sub>. Indeed its constituent roots all have the same distinct outer source and target, and forward inner displacement between zero and <i>d</i>. A commutator can move the source endpoint left by at most <i>d</i>, or the target right by at most <i>d</i>. After a source move the new outer source cannot match another constituent’s target; after a target move the new outer target cannot match another constituent’s source. Each endpoint moves at most once.')
    p('There is no opposite-root interaction: initially the backward separation exceeds <i>b</i> − 2<i>r</i>δ, and after one endpoint move it still exceeds <i>b</i> − (2<i>r</i> + 1)δ &gt; δ. This is greater than or equal to every forward displacement. A root after both moves commutes with all constituents. Thus all corrections stay in the enlarged strip, with the appropriate signs in reverse commutators.')
    p('Put <i>D</i><sub>&gt;h</sub> = <i>D</i><sub>h+1</sub> ··· <i>D</i><sub>m</sub>. The exact collection identity is')
    eq('∏<sub>h=1</sub><sup>m</sup> (<i>D</i><sub>h</sub><i>F</i><sub>h</sub>)<br/>= (<i>D</i><sub>1</sub> ··· <i>D</i><sub>m</sub>) ∏<sub>h=1</sub><sup>m</sup> (<i>D</i><sub>&gt;h</sub><sup>−1</sup><i>F</i><sub>h</sub><i>D</i><sub>&gt;h</sub>).')
    p('Every final correction belongs to <i>V</i><sub>m+1</sub>. Therefore <i>W</i><sub>v</sub>(<i>C</i><sub>b</sub>) = <i>W</i><sub>v</sub>(<i>S</i><sub>b</sub>)<i>u</i> with <i>u</i> in a faithful strip. Both evaluations have identity matrix image, so <i>u</i> does also and equals one. This proves the claimed identity.')

    page('14. The relative torsion theorem')
    p('<b>Theorem 14.1.</b> <i>Let R = ⊕<sub>a≥0</sub>R<sub>a</sub> be a nonnegatively graded unital F<sub>p</sub>-algebra with R<sub>0</sub> = F<sub>p</sub>. Every element of</i>')
    eq('<i>J</i>(<i>R</i>) ∩ ker[St(<i>R</i>) → St(F<sub>p</sub>) ]')
    p('<i>has finite p-power order. Neither matrix nilpotence nor finite presentation is needed for this assertion.</i>')
    p('<b>Proof.</b> The homogeneous substitution ∑ <i>r</i><sub>a</sub> ↦ ∑ <i>r</i><sub>a</sub><i>t</i><sup>a</sup> is a unital ring homomorphism <i>R</i> → <i>R</i>[<i>t</i>]. For a fixed finite root word representing <i>z</i> in the displayed kernel it gives a polynomial root word <i>z</i>(<i>t</i>) with identity matrix image. It evaluates to <i>z</i> at 1 and to its degree-zero reduction at 0. Substitution and block roots define <i>W</i><sub>z</sub>(<i>T</i>) on the stable group. Different embeddings of its finite coordinates give the same central element by Lemma 11.3.')
    p('For all sufficiently large integers <i>b</i>, the wrap-removal identity followed by triangular substitution gives')
    eq('<i>W</i><sub>z</sub>(<i>C</i><sub>b</sub>) = <i>W</i><sub>z</sub>(<i>S</i><sub>b</sub>)<br/>= ∏<sub>s</sub> ι<sub>s</sub>(<i>z</i>(0)) = 1.')
    p('The equality <i>z</i>(0) = 1 is in the stable group; each coordinate embedding is a homomorphism there, so no unstated fixed-rank injectivity is used at this step.')
    p('Now choose <i>b</i> = <i>p</i><sup>a</sup> above that threshold. Since <i>C</i><sub>b</sub><sup>b</sup> = 1 and the characteristic is <i>p</i>,')
    eq('(<i>C</i><sub>b</sub> − 1)<sup>b</sup> = <i>C</i><sub>b</sub><sup>b</sup> − 1 = 0.')
    p('A basis adapted to the kernels of this nilpotent operator makes <i>C</i><sub>b</sub> upper triangular with every diagonal entry 1. The compensated numerical basis change described above implements this inside the stable Steinberg group. Both evaluated words belong to its central kernel, so conjugation leaves them equal. Triangular substitution and Lemma 11.3 now give')
    eq('<i>W</i><sub>z</sub>(<i>C</i><sub>b</sub>) = ∏<sub>s</sub> ι<sub>s</sub>(<i>z</i>) = <i>z</i><sup>b</sup>.')
    p('The previous vanishing therefore proves <i>z</i><sup>pᵃ</sup> = 1. □')
    p('Theorems 10.1, 12.1, and 14.1 and Corollary 13.3 provide the four general Steinberg-group steps in Theorem 1.1. Sections 16-22 construct the required algebra and prove stable truncation separation for it.')
